── WHAT A FRESH SESSION MUST KNOW ──
1. SYNC IS WORSE THAN OLD NOTES SAY, and the instrument was why. Every sync number quoted before
   2026-08-23 was ~31 ms optimistic: the oracle carried an exact +18.00 ms detection bias (half a
   30 fps frame + half a 128-sample envelope window) and never measured the VIDEO reference at all
   (13.5 ms) while correcting the audio one. CI now gates the SYMMETRIC metric at ≤90 ms and reads
   40-66 ms. The instrument's own noise floor is ~8-9 ms 1σ — no band below that is measurable.
   O4's job is to bring this to ≤20 ms; re-tighten the band then.
2. THE AUDIO ANCHOR CANNOT SEE INPUT LATENCY. It dates sample 0 from when a worklet batch ARRIVES,
   so device/stream buffering is invisible and audio lands late. Loopback-measured at +128.7 ms
   (sd 0.70); dating from the audio clock instead changes it by 1.1 ms, so this is signal-path, not
   messaging. Production now subtracts the platform-reported track latency (bounded, logged, never a
   fitted constant — a 90 ms fallback was rejected on those grounds in 2026-07).
3. NOTHING IN ARMING MAY AWAIT WITHOUT A DEADLINE. Each step was bounded but the joins were not, and
   that froze recording with a stale "Waiting for microphone…" label. Budgets now bound the joins,
   every durable-worker round trip has a deadline, the record button cancels an arm, and a pagehide
   guard releases devices. Keep it that way.
4. EVIDENCE IS BROWSER-SIDE. Unit tests cover pure logic only; anything about capture, export,
   memory or bundles must be measured in a real browser on the PRODUCTION build. QA on the prod-build
   port only, never the dev server.
4b. EVERY NUMBER BEFORE 2026-08-25 WAS TAKEN ON AN IDLE MACHINE, AND ONE OF THEM WAS WRONG BECAUSE
   OF IT. "Drift is dead" (beta−1 = −0.003 ms/s) is true idle and false under load: a saturated
   machine's AudioContext returned 56.1 s of quanta in 59.2 s of wall, which drifted the composite's
   audio 2.6-3.8 s per 60 s until it was fixed (see STATE). The take a user records beside a game is
   a different regime from the take a gate records on a quiet machine, and `npm run exp -- syncload`
   is the only cell that runs in it. Anything touching audio placement runs it BOTH ways.
5. THE TAIL IS A DRAIN PROBLEM, AND MediaRecorder LIES ABOUT BEING IDLE. The shipped composite used to
   lose 2734 ms off a 4K take; it now loses 43-320 ms, because stop() kills the painting and then
   PROBES the encoder with requestData() until it answers empty. Waiting for the byte flow to go quiet
   instead looks equivalent and is not — under load this recorder emits four chunks in ten seconds, so
   silence is the normal state and means nothing. Anything that stops a MediaRecorder in this codebase
   should assume the same. The band now runs where it can fail: `npm run oracle:load` (HEAVY, two
   phases). THE RAW HALF IS ALSO FIXED (P0-tail-raw) AND ITS LESSON IS THE OPPOSITE ONE: you cannot
   drain a raw channel by ending its track, because Chrome stops a MediaRecorder whose stream has
   gone inactive and takes the backlog with it. Starve the source instead — 1 fps, same frame size —
   and drain. Do not write a new tail claim without running `npm run exp -- o4step2` and
   `npm run exp -- p0tailraw`.
6. THERE ARE TWO LIVE-COMPOSITE ENGINES, AND v2 IS THE DEFAULT (flipped 2026-08-24). v2 = worker +
   WebCodecs + WebGL2 + fMP4 to its own SyncAccessHandle. v1 (MediaRecorder) is the LADDER under
   it, all three rungs real: capability (no TrackProcessor/AudioEncoder → v1 from the start, which
   is all of Apple WebKit and Firefox), start failure (v2 throws → v1 takes the take), mid-take
   watchdog (sustained <12 fps after first output → composite refused, take renders from raw
   channels). `?engine=v1` / localStorage force the old engine. HISTORY THAT WILL SAVE YOU A WEEK:
   three sessions of "WebCodecs delivers 2-10 fps" were the INSTRUMENT — a fresh throwaway
