15. CAPTURE MEASURES THE MIX ONCE AND EVERY CONSUMER SHOULD ASK IT (X1, 2026-08-26). The take's
   100 ms window envelope — RMS and per-window peak, in TIME ORDER, on the recording timeline — is on
   `Recording.loudness.envelope`. A percentile is a statistic of a multiset, so an EDIT is a
   SELECTION of those windows and needs no second decode: `loudnessFromCaptureEnvelope` serves the
   render and smart cut, and analyzeSilence builds an output-grid array from it for F5a. Before
   adding a fourth consumer, read the fences — they are the whole safety argument, and each one
   marks a case where the stored windows describe a signal the file will not carry (a per-channel
   trim, a sped span, a channel set that is not what capture summed, the p20 codec floor). And the
   two ways this family of statistics has already gone wrong are both DROPPED FIELDS, not wrong
   maths: `peakRobust` was computed in two places and copied to neither consumer for a day and a
   half, which silently kept the audio regression PO reported. If you add a statistic here, follow
   it all the way to the thing that reads it.

13. THE EXPORT'S FLOOR IS DECODE, NOT SCHEDULING — measured, and it retires a whole family of ideas.
   O5 assumed the render loop was badly overlapped. It is not: mediabunny's encoder wrapper already
   keeps 4 frames in the VideoEncoder queue, and the expensive part of "decode" is JS on the same
   thread, so it cannot hide behind the draw either. 12 s 1080p edited take: decode-wait 1295 ms,
   draw 450 ms, encode-wait 28 ms — against 962 ms to decode the same frames with no draw and no
   encode at all. An encode lookahead window AND a decoder prefetch pump were both built, measured,
   and deleted for buying nothing. Do not propose "pipeline the export" again without first running
   `npm run exp -- o5` and beating the decode floor it prints. The way past the floor is to decode
   FEWER frames, which is smart cut.
   Two traps in the same area, both paid for already: (a) the FIRST lane of any export matrix pays the
   codec cold start — warm with a discarded export and run both orders, or you will report the second
   engine as faster whichever one it is; (b) compose/scratch.ts deletes the previous finished export
   when a new one finishes, so an earlier ExportResult.blob goes dead and reads as a bare
   `TypeError: network error`. A rig comparing several files must copy each one into memory first.

14. EVERY SYNC NUMBER THIS PROJECT QUOTED BEFORE 2026-08-24 WAS THE RENDER'S, and the render is the
   path a user gets LAST. The oracle exported by calling exportRecording directly, so the instant
   packet copy (what an unedited take takes) and smart cut were invisible to it. They are not any
   more: the rig records a composite, the trim goes through compose/choose.ts — the product's own
   ladder — and every oracle line now prints `inst=` (instant-path sync), `trim=<path>/<sync>` and
   `compT0=` (where the composite's clock starts). The first honest run found the instant path
   outside the band. If you change anything in compose/*, read those three fields, not just `sync=`.
   THE AUDIO-QUALITY HALF OF THE SAME HOLE IS CLOSED NOW TOO (BACKLOG P0, 2026-08-26), and closing
   it REFUTED the premise it was written on. The backlog expected the instant lane to read ~3.1 dB
   down because the live mix applies a shared 0.7 bus and a 12:1 compressor. IT DOES NOT, because
   NO EXPORT PATH CARRIES THE COMPOSITE'S AUDIO: exportInstant copies the composite's VIDEO packets
   and mixes the audio from the RAW channels through the same certified mixer the render uses —
   deliberately, and instant.ts has said so in its opening comment since it was written ("without
   the composite's uncertified MediaRecorder audio, which was the pervasive-noise cause"). Measured
   on one take: raw channel 0.01 dB · the composite's own audio −2.00 dB · the instant export
   −6.07 dB · the render of the same take −6.07 dB, IDENTICAL. So the instant path's audio quality
   IS the render's, the 1/N headroom rule is the whole of its level cost, and the 0.7 bus lives in
   a track every export discards. If you are hunting audio quality, the composite's audio is the
   WRONG file to look at — `npm run oracle:fidelity` prints it as a diagnostic lane precisely so
   nobody re-derives that the hard way.

16. A LEVEL GATE MUST NOT MEASURE A WINDOW ITS SOURCES ARE NOT IN, AND METADATA CANNOT TELL IT
   WHERE THEY ARE (2026-08-26; note 10's sixth instance, and it took THREE tries to actually fix).
   A tone absent for fraction f of the measured window reads (1−f) low across EVERY tone at once —
   the exact shape of a mix-bus regression, which is what makes it dangerous. The fidelity oracle
   read phantom attenuation four different ways before the window was right: 0.5 dB twice, a
   30.2 dB "tone error" with 26.7 dB "separation" that FAILED A GREEN BUILD, and 11.9/16.1 dB once
   the first two fixes were in. Each fix was a smaller wrong idea than the last:
     (a) a FIXED 0.25 s skip — clears encoder delay on a take whose audio runs from t≈0, clears
         nothing on a multi-source take whose channels start at their own offsets (258-624 ms);
     (b) skip past the last channel's `startOffsetMs` — better, and still blind, because a channel
         can be RECORDING SILENCE: the generator's AudioContext stalls before it renders (rig.ts
         says 115-500 ms; observed 2.15 s here), and no metadata anywhere records that;
     (c) FIND THE SIGNAL — `findProgrammeOnsetSec` opens the window once every channel the fixture
         expects is above the floor, plus a ramp. Per CHANNEL, because on a multi-source take the
         two sources stall independently and an onset taken on the mix fires on whichever arrives
         first (that was the 11.9/16.1 dB).
   And then the cheaper half nobody reached for first: `waitForAudioClock` holds until the
   generator is genuinely rendering BEFORE the take starts, so the silence is not recorded at all —
   the same move production makes by warming its encoder at mount. Onsets went from 2.15 s to
   0.12-0.34 s and four consecutive runs read residual 0.01-0.03 dB on both lanes. The seeker stays
   as the net. Every fidelity report now prints `win=<start>s/onset=<found>s`: a level number is
