    levels on 3.8 % of pixels. `oracle/textEdge.ts` (pure, 7 tests) looks ONLY at glyph edges, chosen
    from the SOURCE so a codec that erases an edge cannot also erase the evidence, and separates the
    two ways text dies: `chromaFringeMean` (4:2:0 bleeding colour across a boundary), `lumaSmearMean`
    (the shape blurred) and `edgeContrastKept` (decoded gradient / source gradient, clamped at 1 so
    ringing is not scored as sharpness).
(2) WHAT THIS MACHINE ACTUALLY ACCEPTS — O9(a) and O9(b) in full, probed per acceleration mode:
        AVC High 4:4:4 (avc1.f40028)   NOT SUPPORTED, any mode
        VP9 profile 1 (4:4:4)          NOT SUPPORTED, any mode
        AV1 main                       supported (software)
        AV1 profile 1 = 4:4:4          SUPPORTED (software)
    So "4:4:4 where hardware supports it" resolves to: nowhere in hardware, and only through AV1 in
    software.
(3) THE GATE, on identical source frames straight off the canvas (`npm run exp -- o9`):
        codec              bytes/frame   chromaFringe   lumaSmear   edgeContrastKept
        avc 4:2:0 shipped        9,856           9.45        2.85               1.00
        avc 4:2:0 @24 Mbps      12,441           9.08        1.85               1.00
        vp9 4:2:0               13,257           9.09        2.33               1.00
        AV1 4:4:4                9,155           4.63        3.44               0.99
        AV1 main 4:2:0           7,532           9.95        3.48               1.00
    AV1 4:4:4 HALVES the chroma fringe and costs FEWER bytes than what ships. And the control is the
    part that makes it a finding rather than a coincidence: THREE TIMES THE BITRATE on 4:2:0 moves
    the fringe by 4 % (9.45 → 9.08). The lever is chroma resolution, not bits — which is why nobody
    was ever going to fix this by raising the ceiling.
(4) THIS SECTION WAS WRONG, AND X15(b) SHOWS EXACTLY HOW — 2026-08-26. It said "the export is the
    small half": capture chromaFringe 23.2 · lumaSmear 68.6 · edgeContrastKept 0.49 against the
    export's 5.7 · 2.4 · 1.00, i.e. Chromium's VP9 destroying half of every glyph's edge contrast
    before the export sees a pixel.
    THE CAPTURE ROW IS A MEASUREMENT OF A SCROLL POSITION. `runTextPerfect` samples the decoded
    files at t = 1.0 / 3.5 / 6.0 s and compares them against ONE `canvasFrame(source.canvas)` taken
    AFTER the export finished — and screenLikeSource scrolls a line every 2.5 s and never repeats.
    The code comment asserting "the frame at 1.0 s and at 1.0 s + 2.5·k are the same picture apart
    from the caret" is the bug: `scroll = Math.floor(t / 2.5)` is monotonic, not periodic. The
    EXPORT row was file-against-file at matching instants and was therefore fine, which is why the
    two rows disagree by a factor of 28 — a mean luma error of 68 of 255 on every glyph edge is not
    an encoder, it is a different page.
    RE-MEASURED against the picture that was actually encoded (x15b, deterministic-by-frame-index
    source, compared by ORDINAL):
        capture (canvas → raw channel)   chromaFringe  8.87 · lumaSmear 0.80 · edgeContrastKept 1.00
        export  (raw channel → file)     chromaFringe  3.02 · lumaSmear 1.79 · edgeContrastKept 1.00
        total   (canvas → file)          chromaFringe 10.81 · lumaSmear 1.95 · edgeContrastKept 1.00
    and shifting the reference by ONE SCROLL STEP reproduces 25.62 / 66.28 / 0.51 on demand, which
    is O9's published row. Neither stage destroys glyph contrast. There is no big half.
    WHAT SURVIVES: (3)'s codec table, which was always measured on identical frames and is
    unaffected — AV1 4:4:4 really does halve the fringe at fewer bytes, and 3x the bitrate on 4:2:0
    really does buy 4 %. WHAT DIES: the recommendation's reasoning ("the lever that reaches the big
    half is CAPTURE, which is X6"). X6 encodes AVC 4:2:0 and was never going to help text, and now
    there is no capture-side damage for it to have helped with either.
RECOMMENDATION, and it has a tension in it that PO should see rather than have resolved quietly: the
lever that reaches the big half is CAPTURE, which is X6. But X6 as built encodes AVC 4:2:0, so it
does not fix text either; fixing text at capture means AV1 4:4:4 at capture, which on this machine is
SOFTWARE — the exact CPU cost X6 exists to remove. Flipping the EXPORT to AV1 4:4:4 is the cheap,
real, small win (half the export's fringe, fewer bytes) and it is PO's call because AV1 playback is
not universal — O9's own gate says "every mode plays cross-browser per ladder", and that is the
question to answer before the ladder gains an AV1 rung.
NOT DONE: (c) near-lossless static spans. It is a bitrate-allocation idea, and the measurement above
says allocation is not what text is losing — 3× the bits bought 4 %. It should be re-proposed only
against the codec table, not against intuition.

