## AI1. export-for-ai — one file  [PO priority, medium; READY]
Why: agents don't watch video — they sample frames and pay ~1 token per 750 pixels. PO's bar:
ONE file that ANY AI understands (chat AIs included — no shell, no unzip), not human-watchable,
maximally token-cheap. The one-file format every major AI ingests natively as text+images is PDF;
zip needs a shell, markdown cannot carry images, base64-in-text explodes tokens, mp4 is the thing
agents cannot afford. Everything richer is AI2+ and must not creep into this task.
Goal: one "For AI" control in the export panel → `<take>-for-ai.pdf`:
  PAGE 1 — index, pure selectable text, ≤~200 tokens: duration, resolution, clock note, the event
  lines (v1 = capture facts only: channel on/off segments, stalls — all already in Recording, no
  new capture surface), and the keyframe list (timestamp + page number per line, so a paging agent
  descends selectively).
  THEN one pixel-delta keyframe per page: ≤1024 px full view, JPEG (PDF-native DCTDecode),
  timestamp caption as real text; a full-res crop of the changed region on the same page ONLY when
  that region is small (<~25 % of frame) — signal-derived, never a setting. Page dimensions equal
  the image: no pixel is padding, per-page cost predictable (~800 tokens at 1024×576).
  NO video track anywhere in the file. The ordinary MP4 stays a separate, unrelated export.
Design rules (decisions, recorded in the spec): the file FOLLOWS THE EDIT — keyframe and event
times remapped through the same output→recording mapping the render uses, cut content absent ·
all timestamps on the RECORDING EPOCH with clockOffsetMs declared on page 1 when the source
video's t=0 differs (P0-instant-sync's lesson: composite time is not recording time) · index is
signal-derived, never generated prose (the no-AI rule covers export-time summarization) ·
keyframe selection pure + unit-tested with a defined delta metric (share of changed pixels on a
downscaled luma diff) · THE CURSOR IS FILTERED BY THE DELTA'S OWN SHAPE, not by a knob (PO raised
it: "it moves every frame but not very useful all the time"): tiny+moving+transient = cursor →
never a keyframe, logged as a low-rate pointer trail in the index text (v1's only interaction
signal while DOM events wait on AI2); tiny+stationary+blinking = caret → ignored; small but
PERSISTENT = real content (tooltip, menu, hover) → keyframe with the crop on it; large = content →
keyframes as normal. A change landing where the cursor last rested captions "change at cursor".
The FILTER ships unconditionally (a threshold); the TRAIL ships only if the rig proves the
detector reliable — report precision either way.
Implementation notes: tap composed output frames inside the machinery the render already iterates
(compose/render.ts loop, chosen via choose.ts) rather than building a second decode pipeline —
note 13: decode is the floor, nothing may decode the take twice · a minimal PDF writer is ours
and tiny (JPEG XObjects + built-in Helvetica text, a few hundred lines, zero deps) · write
through scratch (O1) so memory stays flat · src/core owns the builder, the app owns the button.
Constraints: no knobs in the UI · every other export path untouched, defaults unchanged · the
file must parse in strict readers (pdftotext/mutool clean) — "any AI" starts at "valid PDF".
GATES:
· any-AI ingestion proven: pdftotext extracts the full index text · the file opens in a real
  chat-AI upload (PO's test) and page 1 answers "what is this recording" without descending
· token price printed at export: per-page (pixels/750 + text/4) and total, reported for a real take
· keyframe economy: a 60 s mostly-static synthetic take → ≤8 keyframe pages · a 10 s idle span
